{"title":"The Number Of Ways To Make The Sum","difficulty":"Medium","statement":"You have an infinite number of coins with values 1, 2, and 6, and only 2 coins with value 4. Given an integer n, return the number of ways to make the sum of n with the coins you have. Since the answer may be very large, return it modulo 10^9 + 7. Note that the order of the coins doesn't matter and [2, 2, 3] is the same as [2, 3, 2].\n\n## Examples\n\n### Example 1\n\n```text\nInput: n = 4\nOutput: 4\n```\n\nExplanation: Here are the four combinations: [1, 1, 1, 1], [1, 1, 2], [2, 2], [4].\n\n### Example 2\n\n```text\nInput: n = 12\nOutput: 22\n```\n\nExplanation: Note that [4, 4, 4] is not a valid combination since we cannot use 4 three times.\n\n### Example 3\n\n```text\nInput: n = 5\nOutput: 4\n```\n\nExplanation: Here are the four combinations: [1, 1, 1, 1, 1], [1, 1, 1, 2], [1, 2, 2], [1, 4].\n\n## Constraints\n\n- 1 <= n <= 10^5\n\nCount unordered combinations of coins with values 1, 2, and 6 available without limit and at most two coins of value 4. Return the count modulo 1,000,000,007.","starterCode":"class Solution:\n    def numberOfWays(self, n: int) -> int:\n        pass\n","entryPoint":"Solution().numberOfWays","parameters":[{"name":"n","type":"int"}],"returnType":"int","tests":[{"input":[1],"expected":1},{"input":[10],"expected":16},{"input":[100000],"expected":250024994},{"input":[1001],"expected":125251},{"input":[1010],"expected":127766},{"input":[1011],"expected":127766},{"input":[1025],"expected":131329},{"input":[1031],"expected":132871},{"input":[1045],"expected":136504},{"input":[1046],"expected":137027},{"input":[1065],"expected":141779},{"input":[1069],"expected":142846},{"input":[1070],"expected":143381},{"input":[1073],"expected":143917},{"input":[1092],"expected":149332},{"input":[1094],"expected":149879},{"input":[11],"expected":16},{"input":[1113],"expected":154847},{"input":[1117],"expected":155962},{"input":[1120],"expected":157081},{"input":[1135],"expecte